This is from the Cursor blog [1] linked in the description, but repeating it here in case it&#x27;s useful to anyone: ------  Check whether you are impacted  The malicious extension appends the attacker&#x27;s public key, labeled svc-telemetry, to your SSH authorized keys. To check for it: On macOS, run:      grep -qs svc-telemetry ~&#x2F;.ssh&#x2F;authorized_keys &amp;&amp; echo &quot;svc-telemetry key found&quot; || echo &quot;No key found; machine not impacted&quot;

On Windows, run in PowerShell:      $files = &quot;$HOME\.ssh\authorized_keys&quot;, &quot;C:\ProgramData\ssh\administrators_authorized_keys&quot; | Where-Object { Test-Path $_ }
   if ($files -and (Select-String svc-telemetry $files)) { &quot;svc-telemetry key found&quot; } else { &quot;No key found; machine not impacted&quot; }

